            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to sharpen, then the Latin for a stonecutter."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: colus, colī",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "colus, colī — \"a distaff (for spinning)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Avia colum tenet et lānam trahit. Grandmother holds the distaff and draws out the wool."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, with second and fourth declension forms: colus, colī or colūs f. The stick that holds the raw wool."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "colus is inherited from PIE kʷélus, built on kʷel- 'to turn' (EtymDB), the turning stick of the spinner. Lewis & Short suggest a different root, for being bent or round, so the details are not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
