          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: tēla, tēlae",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tēla, tēlae — \"a web on the loom, the warp; a loom\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Fēminae in ātriō ad tēlam labōrant. The women work at the loom in the hall."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "First declension, feminine: tēla, tēlae f. Also a spider's web. French toile 'cloth' comes from it, and so English toilet, first a little cloth."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tēla is built on texō 'to weave' (EtymDB), and Lewis & Short take it as contracted from an older texia, as āla stands beside axilla. French toile 'cloth' comes from it, and English toilet from the French diminutive toilette."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
